{
  "summary": "Added a startup script with hardcoded Postgres.app paths in configuration variables. The script allows environment variable overrides (PSQL_PATH, PG_CTL_PATH, PG_DATA), but the fallback defaults are hardcoded to Postgres.app-specific paths that fail on other systems (Homebrew, Docker, system postgres).",
  "learnings": [
    {
      "topic": "Shell configuration defaults",
      "reminder": "Never hardcode machine-specific paths in shell script defaults. Use 'which psql' or 'command -v psql' to find the first available postgres client on the PATH instead of falling back to a specific path."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Cross-platform database scripts",
      "reminder": "Database connection defaults should detect what's available, not assume a specific installation. Check for 'psql' on PATH first, then try common locations, then fail with helpful error."
    },
    {
      "topic": "Environment variable fallbacks",
      "reminder": "When using ${VAR:-default}, the default should be generic/fail-safe, not specific to one developer's setup. For portable scripts, default to empty and detect or error."
    }
  ],
  "findings": [
    {
      "type": "bug",
      "severity": "high",
      "location": "Lines 15-17: Configuration variables",
      "description": "Hardcoded Postgres.app paths as defaults - fails on Homebrew, Docker, or system postgres installations",
      "details": "PSQL_PATH and PG_CTL_PATH default to Postgres.app 18 specific paths. Users without Postgres.app will get 'command not found' errors."
    },
    {
      "type": "bug",
      "severity": "high",
      "location": "Line 18: PG_DATA path",
      "description": "Hardcoded Postgres.app data directory path",
      "details": "PG_DATA defaults to Postgres.app's specific var-18-2 directory, not portable across installations."
    },
    {
      "type": "bug",
      "severity": "medium",
      "location": "Line 21: DB_USER hardcoded",
      "description": "Database user hardcoded to 'postgres'",
      "details": "Many systems use different default users (Mac system postgres, 'jk', etc). Should either detect from env or prompt."
    },
    {
      "type": "bug",
      "severity": "medium",
      "location": "Line 20: DB_HOST hardcoded",
      "description": "Database host hardcoded to 127.0.0.1",
      "details": "Doesn't account for Unix socket connections (common on Linux) or container networking."
    },
    {
      "type": "issue",
      "severity": "medium",
      "location": "check_postgres() function",
      "description": "No fallback if PSQL_PATH doesn't exist",
      "details": "Script fails immediately if psql binary doesn't exist at hardcoded path, without trying to find it elsewhere."
    }
  ],
  "suggestions": [
    "Replace hardcoded paths with PATH detection: PSQL_PATH=$(command -v psql 2>/dev/null || echo '/Applications/Postgres.app/Contents/Versions/18/bin/psql')",
    "Add a detect_postgres() helper function that tries multiple installation methods",
    "Check if pg_ctl exists next to psql rather than hardcoding separate path",
    "Add --help output showing environment variables users can override",
    "Consider using pg_isready (comes with postgres) to check availability instead of raw psql",
    "Add detection for: command -v psql, Homebrew (/usr/local/bin, /opt/homebrew/bin), Postgres.app, Docker container"
  ],
  "praise": [
    "Good decision to use environment variable overrides (${PSQL_PATH:-...}) - this makes it workable for users who know to set env vars",
    "The structure is good - separate detection and startup functions",
    "Allows PSQL_PATH and PG_CTL_PATH to be overridden via environment"
  ],
  "overallScore": 50,
  "codeQualityScore": 60,
  "testCoverageScore": 0,
  "documentationScore": 70
}
```

